title: '[pallet-assets-precompiles] `approve`/`permit` saturate on `U256 > Balance::MAX`'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: "## Summary\n\n`approve` and `permit` now saturate the *stored* allowance\
    \ at `Balance::MAX` when `call.value > Balance::MAX`, instead of reverting at\
    \ the `U256 → Balance` conversion. MetaMask, Uniswap, and most DEX routers hard-code\
    \ `type(uint256).max` in approval calls — the universal \"infinite allowance\"\
    \ idiom — and the previous behaviour broke every one of those flows on chains\
    \ where the runtime `Balance` (u128 or u64) can't hold `U256::MAX`.\n\nSaturation\
    \ is lossless: total supply is also capped at `Balance::MAX`, so a larger allowance\
    \ would grant no additional spending power.\n\n## Sentinel semantics\n\nA stored\
    \ allowance of exactly `Balance::MAX` is the \"infinite allowance\" sentinel:\n\
    \n- `transferFrom` skips the approval decrement (`do_transfer` instead of `do_transfer_approved`),\
    \ so once a sentinel approval is set, the spender can keep transferring without\
    \ chipping away at it — matching OpenZeppelin's `_spendAllowance` rule on Ethereum\
    \ mainnet.\n- `allowance()` returns `U256::MAX` rather than the stored `Balance::MAX`,\
    \ so EVM wallets and indexers see the canonical \"unlimited\" wire value they\
    \ pattern-match against.\n- The `Approval` event is normalized to `U256::MAX`\
    \ when the write lands on the sentinel, keeping `event.value == allowance()`\
    \ for any sentinel-producing call (whether the caller signed `U256::MAX`,\
    \ `Balance::MAX + 1`, or exactly `Balance::MAX`). Indexers replaying logs see\
    \ the same value the chain would return.\n\nAny subsequent finite write to the\
    \ same `(owner, spender)` pair overwrites `stored` below `Balance::MAX` and\
    \ collapses the sentinel — `transferFrom` decrements normally from then on.\n\n\
    ## Note on out-of-band mutations\n\n`pallet_assets::Approvals` can be mutated\
    \ by callers outside this precompile (`transfer_approved`, `cancel_approval`,\
    \ `force_cancel_approval`, additive `approve_transfer`). Because the sentinel\
    \ is the stored value itself — not a separately-tracked flag — those mutations\
    \ are reflected immediately: a decrement below `Balance::MAX` collapses the\
    \ sentinel for both `allowance()` and `transferFrom`, with no precompile-side\
    \ bookkeeping needed.\n\n## Doc-comment cleanup\n\nThe `approve` doc-comment\
    \ previously told callers to use `increaseAllowance` / `decreaseAllowance` to\
    \ mitigate the well-known approve front-running race, but neither method is\
    \ exposed by this precompile (OpenZeppelin removed them in v5.0). Replaced with\
    \ a pointer to `permit()`, which this precompile *does* expose and which solves\
    \ the same problem atomically via signature.\n\n## Versioning\n\nBumped as **patch**:\
    \ behaviour change is a strict superset of the previous behaviour. Calls that\
    \ previously reverted (`approve`/`permit` with `U256 > Balance::MAX`) now succeed;\
    \ no previously-successful call changes outcome. The Rust crate API surface\
    \ (traits, types, `Config`) is unchanged.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `cargo test\
    \ -p pallet-assets-precompiles` passes"
crates:
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: patch
